Venetium 0052 - webcam on Windows ARM32: a WinRT MediaCapture capture backend (MediaFrameReader, ~30 fps on the probe).
On the ARM32 cameras (Tegra's NVIDIA Unified AvStream, the Lumias) the desktop Media Foundation capture path enumerates and
opens the camera but can never start streaming it: ReadSample fails with MF_E_HW_MFT_FAILED_START_STREAMING (0xC00D3704)
for every native, NV12 and RGB32 type, unsandboxed, with EnableFrameServerMode=0 too (mediaprobe v1/v2, his device,
2026-10-14). getUserMedia therefore failed with "NotReadableError: Could not start video source". WinRT MediaCapture
drives the same camera (TelegArm works on the device), so on ARM32 VideoCaptureDeviceFactoryWin::CreateDevice returns a
new VideoCaptureDeviceWinRT; enumeration (GetDevicesInfo) stays on Media Foundation, which works there.

VideoCaptureDeviceWinRT (media/capture/video/win/video_capture_device_winrt.{h,cc}) runs its own MTA thread:
- picks the camera (DeviceInformation.FindAllAsync(VideoCapture), matching the descriptor's device id), initializes
  MediaCapture with StreamingCaptureMode=Video (then AudioAndVideo), PhotoCaptureSource=Auto and NO MemoryPreference -
  forcing MemoryPreference=Cpu is what makes this camera's hardware MFT refuse to start;
- streams with a MediaFrameReader on the VideoPreview Color source (winrtcamprobe v4 on the Tegra, 2026-10-15: 30 fps on
  both cameras; the VideoRecord source only gives NVIDIA's opaque '420O' surfaces, and a headless StartPreviewAsync fails
  with 0xC00D36BB). SelectSourceFormat picks the closest uncompressed YUY2/NV12 format to the request (default 640x480@30,
  capped at 1280x720, fps not above the request), trying the best 3; the reader is created NV12 first, then native;
- frames arrive as Direct3D surfaces: SoftwareBitmap.CreateCopyFromSurfaceAsync, then LockBuffer +
  IMemoryBufferByteAccess, delivered in place when the planes are already tightly packed, else repacked (bounds-checked,
  even dimensions) as NV12 / YUY2 / ARGB to OnIncomingCapturedData, with one monotonic clock for the timestamps;
- if the reader delivers nothing it falls back to MediaCapture photo capture (LowLagPhotoCapture, else
  CapturePhotoToStreamAsync, MJPEG, ~2 fps) so the camera still works; a stream that stalls (4 s before the first
  frame, 5 s after) or MediaCapture.Failed reports OnError instead of freezing; OnStarted is sent on the first real frame.
WRL + HRESULT only (no C++/WinRT, no exceptions - the ARM32 toolchain has none). Every async operation is awaited on a
shared event together with the device's stop event and a timeout, and cancelled on stop. ARM32 only: BUILD.gn adds the
files and runtimeobject.lib under current_cpu == "arm"; x86/x64 capture is unchanged.
Device: the stream itself is device-measured (probe v4); this Chromium build of the backend is NOT yet device-tested.

